

{"id":"efe7d9db-8ac1-4e57-adc8-9ef033a22f78","object":"chat.completion","created":1786438600,"model":"deepseek-v4-pro","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"record_id\":\"CA12_CORE_016\",\"decisions\":[{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_01\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_03\",\"held\":true,\"path_id\":null,\"depart_slot\":null,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_02\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_05\",\"depart_slot\":10,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_03\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_03\",\"held\":true,\"path_id\":null,\"depart_slot\":null,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_04\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_02\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_02\",\"depart_slot\":21,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_05\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_04\",\"depart_slot\":6,\"charge_blocks\":0},{\"mission_id\":\"MISSION_06\",\"uav_id\":\"UAV_01\",\"held\":false,\"path_id\":\"PATH_08\",\"depart_slot\":17,\"charge_blocks\":0}]}","reasoning_content":"We are given a complex public task for a UAV integrated-repair optimization. The task asks us to produce one complete repair covering every mission exactly once, respecting fixed UAV assignment and mission order, route compatibility, release/due windows, incoming deadhead, charging, SOC/reserve, corridor/pad/charger capacity, closures, and all intervention overrides. Optimize lexicographically: urgent holds, total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy.\n\nWe are given the baseline decisions (candidate solution) and the instance details with interventions. The missions are fixed to UAVs and must be flown in the fixed order per UAV. For each UAV, the missions are in sequence index order. We need to check the baseline decisions against the interventions and constraints, and likely find a repair that improves upon the baseline (or if baseline is already optimal, we might return it). But the task says \"Produce one complete integrated UAV repair for the perturbed scenario.\" So we need to generate a new set of decisions that satisfy all constraints and optimize the lexicographic objective.\n\nWe are given the intervention overrides: initial SOC for UAV_03 is changed from 3.797458 to 2.747458. Also corridor COR_02 is closed from slot 9 onward (slots 9-32). Also pad capacity override: at VP_02, slot 8, capacity becomes 1 (it was 2). No other overrides.\n\nWe need to examine each mission, UAV assignments, and sequence order. Missions are already assigned to UAVs:\n\nUAV_01: MISSION_05 (seq 0), MISSION_02 (seq 1), MISSION_06 (seq 2) in that order.\nUAV_02: MISSION_04 (seq 0).\nUAV_03: MISSION_01 (seq 0), MISSION_03 (seq 1).\n\n(Check: the instance says assignment_order_contract: FIXED_UAV_ASSIGNMENT_AND_FIXED_UAV_ORDER)\n\nThus, we must keep the same UAV for each mission, and the order within each UAV is fixed by sequence_index. The missions are listed with sequence_index, so we need to process them in that order per UAV.\n\nNow, for each mission, we must choose a path among path_options, a departure slot, charge_blocks (0-3), and whether held. Held missions are not flown; if held is true, path_id and depart_slot are null and charge_blocks=0. Held missions count toward total holds (we want to minimize total holds, and urgent holds are missions with priority \"urgent\" but here all are \"routine\"). So no urgent holds; we want to minimize total holds, then repair distance, etc.\n\nWe must respect the normative SOC event order: incoming deadhead (if any) -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge. The missions have no incoming deadhead (incoming_deadhead: null for all). So only service flight energy is consumed, then reserve check (remaining energy must be >= reserve_kwh after subtracting service flight energy). Then at destination, there is a turnaround (turnaround_slots=1 globally). So after arriving at destination, the UAV is unavailable for turnaround_slots (1 slot) before it can start charging. During the turnaround, it's not using charger. After turnaround, it can charge for charge_blocks blocks. Each charge block takes presumably one slot? The instance doesn't specify charge block duration, but from typical usage, charge_blocks means number of charging blocks of length? Looking at fleet: charge_kwh_per_block for each UAV: UAV_01: 1.2 kWh/block, UAV_02: 0.95 kWh/block, UAV_03: 0.95 kWh/block. Max_charge_blocks=3. So charging occurs at the destination after turnaround for the specified number of blocks (takes 1 slot per block?). The slot duration? The instance has horizon_slots=33, slot_min=5, so slots are time units. Likely each charge block occupies one slot. We also have charger capacity constraints: charger_capacity_by_slot_default and overrides. At each vertiport, number of chargers is 2 except where overridden. So we need to track chargers occupied per slot at each vertiport.\n\nThe normative event order says: after service flight and turnaround, post-service charge. The charge_blocks attached to a mission occurs at that mission's destination after its service flight and turnaround. That energy becomes available for later missions of the same UAV. So charging after a mission adds to the UAV's SOC.\n\nWe also have constraints: initial SOC for each UAV at its start_vertiport, available_slot (earliest slot the UAV can start its first mission). For UAV_01, start_vertiport is VP_06, available_slot 6, initial SOC 9.631702. For UAV_02, start VP_02, available 6, initial SOC 8.5. For UAV_03, start VP_06, available 6, initial SOC after override: 2.747458 (was 3.797, now 2.747458). So UAV_03 has lower initial energy.\n\nThe baseline decisions must be checked for feasibility under the interventions. Baseline has: MISSION_01 (UAV_03) depart_slot 6, path PATH_06, charge_blocks 0. PATH_06 is distance 1.152 km, travel_slots 2, energy 0.173952 kWh. Incoming deadhead null. So UAV_03 at slot 6 at VP_06 with SOC 2.747458. After service flight, consumes 0.173952, remaining 2.573506. Reserve check: reserve_kwh for UAV_03 = 2.5. So remaining must be >= 2.5 after service before turnaround? The order: incoming deadhead -> service flight -> reserve check -> destination turnaround -> post-service charge. So after service flight, we check reserve: remaining SOC must be >= reserve_kwh. 2.573506 >= 2.5, okay. Then turnaround: mission ends, UAV arrives at destination VP_02 at slot? Depart slot 6, travel_slots=2, so arrival slot = 6+2 = 8. But need to check pad capacity at origin and destination. The service flight occupies the origin pad at depart slot and the destination pad at arrival slot? Usually, pad capacity constraints: at departure, the UAV needs a pad at origin vertiport for the departure slot (or maybe the whole flight? Probably occupying pad at origin at depart slot, and destination pad at arrival slot (and maybe during turnaround? Turnaround likely occupies a pad as well?). The instance has pad_capacity_by_slot_default = 2 per VP, and intervention: pad_capacity_override at VP_02 slot 8: capacity 1. So VP_02 has only 1 pad at slot 8. Mission 01 arrives at VP_02 at slot 8. That's a potential conflict.\n\nAlso, MISSION_03 (UAV_03) sequence_index 1 after MISSION_01. Baseline: MISSION_03 depart_slot 10, path PATH_03, charge_blocks 0. Mission_03 is from VP_02 to VP_05, travel_slots 1. Origin VP_02, departure slot 10. Also, after MISSION_01, UAV_03 is at VP_02. There is turnaround of 1 slot after MISSION_01. So arrival at VP_02 at slot 8, turnaround takes slot 9? Let's think: Arrival slot 8. At slot 8, the UAV lands and is on the pad, then turnaround 1 slot (maybe meaning it occupies the pad for one slot after arrival for turnaround, then frees up?). Standard interpretation: the flight occupies the pad at origin at depart slot, and at destination from arrival slot through turnaround duration? Usually, pad occupation: at origin, from depart_slot to depart_slot + travel_slots - 1? No, the UAV is in flight during travel slots, so pad occupancy at origin is only at the departure slot? Or maybe the UAV occupies a pad only during ground time (turnaround, charging). Typically, a pad is needed when the UAV is on the ground (arrival, turnaround, charging). Let's check typical repair tasks: pad_capacity constraints apply to ground presence. The best approach: pad at origin is needed at depart_slot? Could be that the UAV occupies the origin pad from the start of the mission? Actually, the UAV starts at origin vertiport, and it may have been charging there before departure. But for a mission, the UAV's steps: at the origin, before departure, it may have been present due to previous mission's charging/turnaround. For the first mission, the UAV is at its start_vertiport at the start slot, and it must have a pad available to depart from? The baseline decisions don't seem to violate pad capacity with original? But we have the intervention: pad capacity at VP_02 slot 8 is 1. So we must ensure only one UAV is on the ground at VP_02 at slot 8. In baseline, MISSION_01 arrives at VP_02 at slot 8. Also, other missions? MISSION_05 is UAV_01, path PATH_04, depart 6, travel 2, so it arrives at VP_02 at slot 8 as well (VP_06 to VP_02). Let's check: MISSION_05 destination VP_02, depart slot 6, travel 2, arrival slot 8. So both MISSION_01 and MISSION_05 arrive at VP_02 at slot 8. Each would need a pad at slot 8 for arrival and turnaround? If pad capacity at VP_02 slot 8 is 1, then you can't have two UAVs arrive at the same slot. That likely makes baseline infeasible. The baseline might have been computed before the pad override; now the pad override is binding.\n\nSo baseline is infeasible due to pad capacity. We need to repair.\n\nWe must respect the fixed order per UAV. So we cannot change the assignment or order. We can only change departure slots, path choices, charge_blocks, and hold decisions.\n\nGoal: minimize lexicographically: urgent holds (none), total holds, repair distance, weighted tardiness, delay plus charge wait, then energy. So we want to avoid holds if possible, but if we must hold a mission to make it feasible, we might hold one. But we should first see if we can adjust departure slots and paths to avoid the pad capacity conflict.\n\nMission 01 and Mission 05 both arrive at VP_02 at slot 8 with baseline paths. Can we shift one of them? Both have release slot 6. UAV_01 available slot 6, UAV_03 available slot 6. Could we delay any? For UAV_03, Mission 01 release 6, due 12. Travel slots: PATH_06 =2, PATH_01 =8. If we delay departure beyond slot 6, arrival would be later. For UAV_01, Mission 05 release 6, due slot 8! So it must be completed by slot 8. Travel slots=2, so it must depart at or before slot 6 to arrive by slot 8? Let's compute: mission due slot is the latest arrival slot allowed? \"due_slot\" typically means the mission must be completed (arrival) no later than that slot. So for Mission 05, due_slot=8, travel_slots=2. If depart at slot 6, arrival slot=8 (okay). If depart at slot 7, arrival slot=9, which exceeds due_slot 8. So Mission 05 must depart at slot 6 or earlier? Release slot is 6, so earliest depart is 6. So it must depart slot 6 to be on time. Can we hold Mission 05? Holding means mission not flown, which counts as a hold, and we want to minimize total holds. Holding a mission with due_slot 8 is a late mission? Holding might be allowed, but it's a hold, increasing total holds. Since the lexicographic first objective after urgent holds is total holds, we want to avoid holds if possible. So we'd rather not hold Mission 05. But if we cannot avoid the pad conflict without holding, we might have to hold. Can we avoid by using a different path for Mission 05? Mission 05 only has one path option: PATH_04. So path is fixed. So UAV_01 must depart at slot 6 and arrive at VP_02 at slot 8. So that arrival is fixed.\n\nNow, UAV_03 Mission 01: due_slot 12, release 6, two paths: PATH_06 travel 2, arrival slot 8 if depart 6; PATH_01 travel 8, arrival slot 14 if depart 6 (depart 6+8=14 exceeds due 12). If depart 6, travel 8 gives arrival 14 >12, so not allowed because due 12. So PATH_01 can't be used if depart 6. Could we delay departure to later than 6? The due slot is arrival <= due. For PATH_01 travel 8, we need arrival <=12, so depart <= 4, but release is 6, so can't use PATH_01 at all because earliest depart is 6, arrival 14 >12. So PATH_01 is infeasible. So Mission 01 must use PATH_06 and depart at 6 to meet due slot (arrival 8). So arrival at VP_02 at slot 8 is also fixed for UAV_03. Thus, both missions must arrive at slot 8, causing pad capacity conflict (needs 2 pads, only 1 available). So it's impossible to fly both without holding one. So we must hold at least one mission. We can hold Mission 01 or Mission 05 or both. Holding Mission 05 would be a hold, but Mission 05 is a high priority? Both are routine, no urgent. Since we want to minimize total holds, we should hold exactly one mission, not both. But holding one mission will make the other feasible. However, if we hold Mission 01, then UAV_03's first mission is not flown; but we must still fly Mission 03? The order: Mission 01 is seq 0, Mission 03 is seq 1. If Mission 01 is held, then Mission 03 is still seq 1. Do we have to fly Mission 03? The fixed order means that the UAV performs its missions in order, but if a mission is held, it is skipped. The sequence of flown missions for that UAV would just skip the held one. That is allowed; holding is a decision. So we could hold Mission 01, and then UAV_03 will fly Mission 03 from its start vertiport VP_06? No, if Mission 01 is held, where does UAV_03 start for the next mission? The UAV's initial position is VP_06. After holding Mission 01, does it stay at VP_06? Probably, because no movement occurs. The UAV remains at its previous location. Since no mission flown, it stays at VP_06. Then for Mission 03 which origin is VP_02, but UAV is at VP_06, it would need to deadhead? But Mission 03's expected incoming origin is VP_02, and incoming_deadhead is null. Actually, the \"expected_incoming_origin_vertiport\" is the vertiport from which the mission expects the UAV to come. However, under holding, we might need to consider if the UAV can reposition. The task says \"fixed UAV assignment and mission order\". It doesn't say the UAV must start a mission from the origin at the beginning of the mission. Usually, the UAV must be at the mission's origin vertiport at the departure slot. For the first mission (seq 0) of a UAV, it starts at its start_vertiport. If the mission origin does not match start_vertiport, there is an incoming deadhead path (registered) that can be used? Actually, the missions have an \"incoming_deadhead\" field that can be null or a path. For MISSION_01, incoming_deadhead is null, but origin is VP_06, which matches UAV_03's start vertiport. So that's fine. For MISSION_03, origin VP_02, but if we held MISSION_01, UAV_03 is still at VP_06. We cannot fly MISSION_03 from VP_06 because its origin is VP_02 and there is no incoming deadhead path to reposition from VP_06 to VP_02. The UAV would need to travel from VP_06 to VP_02 before MISSION_03. But the mission's incoming deadhead is null, meaning no repositioning path is provided. In such tasks, typically the UAV must be at the mission's origin at the start, and if previous mission ended at that origin, it's fine. If not, there needs to be a deadhead leg. But for a mission whose preceding mission was held, the UAV's location might not match. The rules might be: if a mission is held, the UAV does not fly it, and for the next mission, the UAV must still be at the expected origin? I'm not sure. Possibly holding a mission doesn't change the UAV's location because it never flew the previous mission that would have taken it elsewhere. But we need to ensure that the UAV's location before a mission is its origin vertiport. For UAV_03's sequence: start VP_06 -> Mission 01 (origin VP_06) -> then if Mission 01 flown, arrives VP_02, then Mission 03 origin VP_02. If Mission 01 held, UAV stays at VP_06, so it cannot do Mission 03 because origin is VP_02 and no deadhead. The instance might allow repositioning via deadhead if there was a registered incoming deadhead path, but here it's null. So holding MISSION_01 would make MISSION_03 impossible without deadhead. Similarly, holding MISSION_05 (UAV_01) would affect MISSION_02 and MISSION_06 since UAV_01's sequence: start VP_06 -> MISSION_05 (origin VP_06) -> MISSION_02 (origin VP_02) -> MISSION_06 (origin VP_01). If MISSION_05 held, UAV_01 stays at VP_06, then MISSION_02 origin VP_02, no deadhead, so cannot. So holding the first mission of a UAV's sequence that doesn't match the next mission's origin might break the sequence. But maybe the UAV can deadhead implicitly? There is no explicit deadhead path unless the mission's expected incoming origin matches? The deadhead contract is FIXED_REGISTERED_INCOMING_DEADHEAD_PATH, meaning incoming deadhead is fixed as registered, which for all missions is null. So no deadhead. Thus, the UAV must be at the mission's origin at the start without any deadhead. Therefore, the UAV's location after previous mission must be that origin. So holding a mission that would have moved the UAV would cause a location mismatch. However, holding a mission that is the last in sequence is fine. But here, if we hold Mission 01, UAV_03 remains at VP_06, but Mission 03 requires origin VP_02 — mismatch. Thus, we cannot hold Mission 01 unless we also hold Mission 03 (or deadhead somehow, but no deadhead). Since total holds would increase, we might need to hold both missions for UAV_03 if we hold Mission 01. Could we hold Mission 03 instead? That doesn't solve the pad conflict because Mission 01 still arrives at slot 8. The conflict is between Mission 05 and Mission 01 arrival. So to resolve conflict, we must prevent one from arriving at slot 8. The only way without holding is to delay one of them, but as argued, Mission 05 must depart at 6 to meet due 8. Could we hold Mission 05? That would leave UAV_01 at VP_06, then next mission MISSION_02 origin VP_02, mismatch. So if we hold Mission 05, we also cannot fly MISSION_02 and MISSION_06 because UAV_01 would be at wrong location. So we'd have to hold all of UAV_01's missions. That's many holds, not good.\n\nThus, maybe there is another solution: we could change the path of Mission 01 to PATH_01? But earlier we saw PATH_01 travel 8, due 12: if depart at 6, arrival 14 >12, not allowed. Could we change departure slot of Mission 01 to later? Release is 6, so cannot depart before 6. To meet due 12 with travel 8, need depart <=4, impossible. So PATH_01 never feasible. So Mission 01 must use PATH_06 and depart 6. So arrival 8 is fixed. So to resolve pad conflict, we must adjust Mission 05. But Mission 05 only one path, travel 2, due 8, release 6, so must depart 6. So it seems impossible to have both flights without holding. Therefore, we must hold at least one mission to avoid pad capacity violation. Which one? The objective minimizes total holds first, then repair distance, etc. If we hold one mission, total holds =1. If we hold more, total holds >1, so 1 hold is better than 2+ holds. Can we achieve a feasible schedule with exactly 1 hold? Let's explore.\n\nThe conflict is arrival at VP_02 at slot 8. If we hold Mission 05, then UAV_01's first mission is not flown, but then we have location mismatch for MISSION_02 (origin VP_02, UAV at VP_06). Could UAV_01 reposition to VP_02 by some means? It could use a deadhead path? The incoming deadhead for MISSION_02 is null, so no. So MISSION_02 cannot be flown. Then MISSION_06 origin VP_01, also mismatch. So we'd have to hold all UAV_01 missions, total holds =3 (missions 05,02,06). Plus maybe Mission 01 still flies. That's 3 holds. Alternatively, hold Mission 01, then UAV_03 cannot fly Mission 03 because origin mismatch, so we'd have to hold Mission 03 too, total holds=2. That's better than 3. So hold Mission 01 and Mission 03 would be 2 holds. Can we do 1 hold? Could we hold Mission 03 only? That doesn't solve pad conflict at slot 8, because Mission 01 still flies. So conflict remains, infeasible. So minimum holds might be 2 if we hold both missions of UAV_03. Could we instead hold Mission 02? Not involved in the conflict. To resolve VP_02 slot 8 pad conflict, only two missions arrive at that time: M01 and M05. So holding one of them is necessary. Holding M05 leads to cascade holds for UAV_01 (since it can't do later missions without deadhead). Holding M01 also leads to cascade for UAV_03's M03. So 2 holds seems best (M01 and M03). What about holding M05 and then somehow repositioning UAV_01 from VP_06 to VP_02 without a deadhead? The task says incoming deadhead is fixed registered; there is no deadhead. So impossible. So indeed, we have to hold both M01 and M03, or hold M05, M02, M06. 2 holds is better than 3 holds. So the repair with least holds would be to hold MISSION_01 and MISSION_03 (total holds 2). Check if the remaining missions (M05, M02, M06, M04) can be flown feasibly.\n\nLet's verify the feasibility of that schedule: UAV_01 flies M05, M02, M06; UAV_02 flies M04. UAV_03 holds both missions. We need to check all constraints: SOC, corridor closures, pad/charger capacities, etc.\n\nFirst, check UAV_01:\n- MISSION_05 (seq 0): depart from VP_06, path PATH_04 (only one), travel 2, energy 0.131702 kWh. Release 6, due 8. Must depart at 6 to arrive slot 8. So depart_slot 6. UAV_01 initial SOC 9.631702 at VP_06 slot 6. After service flight, SOC = 9.631702 - 0.131702 = 9.5. Reserve check: reserve_kwh = 1.5. 9.5 >= 1.5, OK. Arrival at VP_02 at slot 8. Turnaround 1 slot (slot 9). Destination turnaround occupies pad? The normative order says destination turnaround after service flight, then post-service charge. Turnaround likely occupies a pad at the destination for 1 slot. So at VP_02, pad occupancy for UAV_01: arrives slot 8, occupies pad during slot 8 (landing) and turnaround slot 9? Possibly pad needed for arrival slot and turnaround. The baseline pad capacity override at VP_02 slot 8 is 1. If only UAV_01 arrives, it's okay (1 pad used). After turnaround, the UAV can depart for next mission. No post-service charging for M05? The baseline had charge_blocks 0. Could we charge? charge_blocks can be 0-3. If we charge, we need a charger slot. We'll evaluate later. For now assume charge_blocks 0.\n\n- MISSION_02 (seq 1): origin VP_02, due 26, release 9, path options: PATH_05 (COR_04), PATH_07 (COR_02), PATH_09 (COR_01). Path travel slots all 2. The UAV is at VP_02 after M05. It needs a turnaround before next? Actually, after turnaround of M05, UAV is at VP_02 and ready for next mission. The turnaround of M05 ends at slot 9 (if arrival at 8, turnaround slot 9? Arrival slot 8, turnaround takes 1 slot: during slot 9? The turnaround time might be measured in slots. The instance has turnaround_slots=1. So after arriving at slot 8, it cannot depart until slot 10? Let's define: The service flight occupies the UAV from depart_slot to arrival_slot inclusive? Travel_slots is the number of slots from departure to arrival. If travel_slots=2, departure at slot 6, arrival at slot 8? Usually, if travel_slots=2, the flight occupies departure slot and the next slot, arriving at the end of slot 7 or start of slot 8? In many discrete-time formulations, a flight leaves at depart slot, and arrives at depart_slot + travel_slots. So if depart 6, travel 2, arrival slot = 8. At slot 8, the UAV is at destination. The turnaround then occurs at the destination, taking 1 slot. So the UAV is unavailable for departure until turnaround completes. If turnaround takes slot 9, the UAV is ready at slot 10. So the earliest departure for the next mission from VP_02 is slot after turnaround, i.e., slot 9+1? Actually, careful: if arrival slot is 8, the UAV is on the ground at the end of slot 8? For simplicity, assume that turnaround occupies the slot immediately following arrival. So if arrival at slot t, turnaround uses slot t+1, and the UAV becomes available at slot t+2. So after arrival at 8, turnaround at 9, available at 10. So next mission can depart at earliest slot 10. But mission 02 release slot is 9, so cannot depart before release. So depart slot could be 10 or later. Also due slot 26, so plenty of time.\n\nNow, corridor closures: COR_02 is closed from slot 9 onward. So PATH_07 (COR_02) is unavailable if we depart at slot 10 or later (since the flight will travel during slots 10-11? The flight's edge has offset_slot 0, duration_slots 2, so if depart at 10, it occupies corridor at slots 10 and 11? Since corridor COR_02 is closed from slot 9 onward, that means it's closed at slots 9,10,11,... So PATH_07 is not allowed unless we can depart at a slot where the flight does not use the corridor during closed slots. The earliest possible depart to use COR_02 without hitting closure would be before slot 9. But we cannot depart before 9 because mission 02 release is 9, and UAV available after turnaround at 10. So PATH_07 is impossible. So choose between PATH_05 (COR_04, wind 1, energy 0.120413) and PATH_09 (COR_01, wind 1, energy 0.421445). Both are compatible, wind level 1 OK for UAV_01 (max wind 1). Travel slots 2. Depart slot: earliest is 10. Also need to consider due slot 26, so arrival at 12 earliest, fine. So we can choose PATH_05 (lower energy) or PATH_09. We want to minimize energy? Lexicographic objective has \"energy\" last, but we also need to meet energy constraints and overall optimization. We'll need to see if UAV_01 has enough SOC to complete all missions and any charging. So I'll pick PATH_05 for now (lower energy consumption) but need to check if any corridor capacity or pad capacity issues later.\n\n- MISSION_06 (seq 2): origin VP_01, due 29, release 17, path PATH_08 (travel 1, energy 0.294761). After M02, UAV arrives at VP_01. With M02 path PATH_05, depart 10, arrival 12 (depart+2). Then turnaround at VP_01 takes 1 slot (slot 13), UAV available at slot 14. Mission 06 release 17, so earliest depart 17. So we can schedule it. Need to check VP_01 pads/chargers, etc.\n\nNow UAV_02:\n- MISSION_04 (seq 0): origin VP_02, due 28, release 21, path PATH_02 (travel 2, energy 0.160815). UAV_02 start VP_02, available slot 6, initial SOC 8.5. Need to depart at or after release 21. So departure slot >=21. Since due 28, arrival <=28. Travel 2, so depart <=26. So we can pick slot 21, arrival 23. Then turnaround at VP_03 till slot 24. Fine. Charge_blocks? We can consider charging. \n\nNow we must check if holding M01 and M03 is allowed. The decisions for those: held=true, path_id null, depart_slot null, charge_blocks 0.\n\nNow, we need to verify pad capacities for the flown missions. \n- VP_06: at slot 6, UAV_01 departs for M05. Pad capacity at VP_06 is 2 default. At slot 6, only one departure, so fine.\n- VP_02: \n  - UAV_01 M05 arrival slot 8, turnaround slot 9. Pad occupancy at slot 8 and 9? Pad capacity at VP_02 slot 8 is only 1 (override). So only one UAV can be on pad at slot 8. UAV_01 is there. That's okay.\n  - Later, UAV_02 M04 departs from VP_02 at slot 21. That will need a pad at VP_02 at departure slot 21. Pad capacity at VP_02 default 2 (no override for that slot). At slot 21, no conflict with other missions, fine.\n- VP_01: M02 arrival slot 12, turnaround slot 13. Pad capacity default 2, no override.\n- VP_03: M04 arrival slot 23, turnaround slot 24. Pad capacity 2, fine.\n- VP_04: M06 arrival slot 18 (if depart 17, travel 1, arrival 18). Turnaround 19. Fine.\n\nCharger capacities: if we set charge_blocks=0 for all missions, no chargers used, fine.\n\nCorridor capacities: default 2 per slot, no overrides. So should be fine.\n\nNow, check energy constraints across missions for UAV_01 with the chosen paths:\n\nUAV_01 initial SOC 9.631702.\n- M05: depart 6, energy 0.131702, SOC after service = 9.5. Reserve check OK (reserve 1.5). No charging at VP_02 after M05? We could charge, but if we charge, that would occupy charger and may affect slot timing. If we charge, the turnaround slot 9 is used, then we can charge for up to 3 blocks. But charging block likely takes 1 slot per block. So if we set charge_blocks=1, the UAV would charge for 1 slot after turnaround, i.e., slot 10, then would be available at slot 11. That could shift departure for M02 from 10 to 11 (or later). But there's no pad conflict and corridor P05 is fine. However, we need to see if charging is beneficial. The objective has \"delay plus charge wait\" and \"energy\". We want to minimize delay plus charge wait. Charging might add wait time but also increase energy, which could allow less energy consumption later? But energy is last objective, so we should try to minimize earlier objectives first, total holds is 2, then repair distance, then weighted tardiness, then delay+charge wait, then energy. So after fixing holds, we should minimize repair distance. Repair distance is the total distance flown by all UAVs? Or is it the total distance of all missions (flown)? The baseline had specific distance. Our new schedule will have missions M05, M02, M06, M04. So total distance = sum of path distances. We can try to choose paths that minimize distance. But M05 only one path, M06 only one, M04 only one. M02 has two feasible paths (PATH_05 and PATH_09). PATH_05 distance 0.96 km, PATH_09 distance 3.36 km. So we should pick PATH_05 for lower distance, as long as it's feasible. So that reduces repair distance. So we can set path PATH_05 for M02.\n\nWeighted tardiness: tardiness is max(0, arrival_slot - due_slot). For each mission, we compute tardiness. Since none are tardy with our slots (M05 arrival 8=due 8, M02 arrival 12 < due 26, M06 arrival 18 < due 29, M04 arrival 23 < due 28). So no tardiness. So weighted tardiness=0.\n\nDelay plus charge wait: delay might be difference between departure slot and release slot? Or is it waiting time